  "query_preview": "..\n(Late Sunday afternoon through Friday) \nIssued at 225 AM EST Sat Nov 28 2026\n\nLate Sunday afternoon will be the start of more active weather\nwith an incoming cold front and precipitation starting to spread \nacross the area. A potent trough will dig out of the Great Plains\ntowards the Southeast throughout the day. Surface mass response \nto the left exit region of a potent jet streak will lead to \nsurface low deepening across the Midwest that will drag cold front\ntowards the CWA by late Sunday afternoon and evening.\n\nThe kinematics with this system remain impressive. Models \nconsistently show 80+ kt jet streak approaching the CWA by Sunday \nevening with 50-60 kt LLJ ahead of a cold front. Veering profiles \nin the low levels make for some impressive SRH values and scary \nlooking hodographs. However, the limiting factor here still looks \nto be the thermodynamics, which remain extremely low thanks to \nsurface Ts hovering in the 60s in most areas across north and \ncentral Georgia. Even in CAMs like the RRFS which can capture the \nevent, MUCAPE values struggle to reach 100 J/kg with profiles that\nare stable near the surface and mostly moist adiabatic aloft. \nWith most model convection not moving in till after 00Z, the sun \nwill also be set, though WAA and mixing from winds will likely \nprevent a deeper surface inversion for forming. Overall, this \nmeans the likely driver of any convection in the system will be \nthe lift of the front itself, which will make for very shallow-\ntopped convection. The severe potential remains low, but not zero,\nand will be highest across far Northwest Georgia. SPC has expanded\na Marginal Risk (Level 1 of 5) for severe weather into this area.\nThe line or broken line of storms will be capable of mixing down \nthe stronger winds aloft which could lead to some isolated \ndamaging wind gusts even as the system moves through our area.\n\nFront clears the area by Monday morning and rain comes to an end.\nTotal rainfall amounts will vary across the region, with most \nlikely amounts of up to 0.25-0.5\" in central GA, and 0.5-1\" in \nnorth GA. Localized higher totals to 2\" are possible in far north\nGA. Temperatures are quick to rebound through the work week as \nsurface high that builds in rapidly shifts to the east and small \nridge builds in aloft. Highs in the 50s and low 60s on Monday \nbecome widespread 60s by Tuesday. By Wednesday, some areas of \ncentral Georgia may approach 70 for highs. Skies remain mostly \nclear and no rain chances through this time period.\n\nOn the very backend of the long term, another system looks like it \nshould impact the area with high levels of ensemble support for some \nmeasurable rainfall across most of Georgia thanks to deep trough \nover the 4 corners region that progresses to the east by Thursday \ninto Friday of next week. Wouldn't normally talk too much about \nthis, but there is some signal showing up for potentially heavy \nrainfall with this system as it is able to tap into some deeper sub-\ntropical moisture. Ensemble probabilities of 24 hour precip being \ngreater than 1\" is > 50% over north Georgia in GEFS and runs a \nlittle lower within AI Euro ensemble, but still around 20%. An inch \nmay not seem like much, but having confidence levels of at least \nthat much 7+ days out shows considerable ensemble consistency for \nheavier rain, especially in models that are not convective allowing. \nWorth monitoring this system over the coming days to see how the \nmodel probability space continues to evolve.",
